# Going Beyond Prompt Libraries

Prompt libraries are a great way to explore how AI can support teaching and learning. They provide ready-to-use examples that can help you get started and discover what's possible.

However, no prompt library can cover every classroom, subject, or situation. The most effective prompts are often tailored to your students, content, goals, and context.

To get the most value from AI, it's important to move beyond copying prompts and begin adapting and creating your own. This allows you to:
* Tailor AI responses to your specific students and learning goals
* Ask more precise and meaningful questions
* Get higher-quality, more relevant outputs

Rather than thinking of prompts as fixed templates, think of prompting as a skill. Like any skill, it improves with practice.

<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 <strong>Prompt Engineering:</strong> A technique that involves refining and adjusting the inputs given to AI models to achieve more accurate or desired outputs.</p>
</div>

There are many strategies for writing effective prompts. One simple prompt engineering structure you can use is **TaCo FoRT**.

